<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adds a "Kitsu" shelf tab with two buttons — the closest Houdini
     equivalent of the Maya version's "Kitsu/Zou" menu:
       - "Kitsu" opens the panel as a small floating window the artist can
         drag wherever suits them, INCLUDING onto a pane to dock it as a
         tab if they want. This is a deliberate product decision from
         artist feedback, not a technical fallback: Houdini users have
         wildly different pane layouts, so there is no pane this tool can
         pick that is the right answer for everyone. Earlier versions
         tried to dock into a "sensible" pane (the pane under the cursor,
         the first pane on the desktop, an existing tab elsewhere) and it
         went wrong in both directions: docking somewhere unwanted for
         some artists, and, when the chosen pane happened to be collapsed
         on a split or on a hip file's other saved desktop, creating a
         perfectly real tab that nobody could see, which is
         indistinguishable from the button doing nothing at all.
         Uses createFloatingPanel(), NOT createFloatingPaneTab(). This
         matters and is easy to get wrong: per the HOM docs,
         createFloatingPaneTab() builds a deliberately locked-down window
         where "the graphical interface to add more tabs or split the pane
         inside the panel is not exposed" and which "does not contain any
         panes" (hou.PaneTab.pane() returns None on it). That window has
         no tab strip at all, so there is physically nothing to grab to
         drag it onto a pane, and no amount of showPaneTabs() can add one.
         createFloatingPanel() by contrast "is not locked down, and the
         user can add more pane tabs and split the panes", so its tab
         strip is a real, draggable handle. Its pane also gets
         showPaneTabs(True) below, since Houdini can otherwise hide that
         strip when only one tab is in it, which would leave the artist
         with the same "nothing to grab" problem for a different reason.
         Note dragging the window's OS title bar only moves the window; it
         is the little "Kitsu" tab that docks it.
         CAVEAT worth keeping in mind if a layout bug is ever reported
         against this: an early version of this plugin used
         createFloatingPanel() and it "was observed shoving the whole
         Houdini main window to a different screen position when invoked
         (worst over Remote Desktop)". That call passed no position or
         size at all and let Houdini choose, which is exactly the case the
         explicit screen-aware geometry below is meant to avoid, so this
         is believed (not proven) to be addressed. hou.Desktop's
         "immediate" argument, documented as helping "setting the window
         position correctly", is the next lever to try if it recurs.
         Clicking again brings an already-open, reachable panel to the
         front rather than piling up duplicates. "Reachable" is guarded
         the same way the old docking code had to be (pane exists, on the
         current desktop, not collapsed on a split): re-focusing an
         unreachable tab silently succeeds while looking, again, exactly
         like a dead button. A panel can still legitimately be opened more
         than once via any pane's "+" New Pane Tab Type menu, which is why
         panel.py tracks a list rather than a single instance.
         Two further details are copied from SideFX's own floating Python
         Panel code ($HH/python3.11libs/scenegraphlayers/panelutils.py,
         openPanelForNode()), both verified against that source:
           * the interface name goes into the creation call itself rather
             than a separate setActiveInterface() afterward. Both are
             window/OpenGL-context operations (listed in _ui_thread_funcs
             in $HH/python3.11libs/houpythonportion/ui.py) and doing two
             back to back is the leading suspect for the reported glitch
             where Houdini's MAIN window kept drawing from stale layout
             geometry with its whole top chrome row missing (the menu bar,
             which on Windows also carries minimize/maximize/close) until
             any resize forced a relayout. Passing the name up front does
             it in one operation, and additionally suppresses the generic
             Python Panel toolbar a default-constructed one comes with.
           * an explicit position and size on whichever screen the cursor
             is on. Houdini's default placement can land the window on a
             different monitor, and on a mixed-DPI Windows setup that
             forces a UI-scale recompute of the entire interface.
         The work is wrapped in hdefereval.executeDeferred() so the new
         window and its GL context are built after Houdini finishes
         dispatching this shelf click rather than partway through it. Note
         the originally documented reason for this (that the
         _ui_thread_funcs decorator itself needs it) does NOT hold up:
         that decorator (_runInUIThread) is a plain same-thread
         passthrough when already called from the main thread, which a
         shelf click always is, and panelutils.openPanelForNode() calls
         these functions fully synchronously. It is kept as a low-cost
         precaution against Qt reentrancy, creating and activating a
         top-level window from inside a toolbar button's own event
         dispatch being a plausible (if unproven) route to the
         stale-relayout glitch above.
       - "Publish Capture" exports a full-scene H.264 MP4 of the current
         playback range and publishes it to Kitsu as a new revision,
         reusing the open panel's already-resolved login/task state. -->
<shelfDocument>
  <toolshelf name="kitsu_houdini_plugin" label="Kitsu">
    <memberTool name="kitsu_open_panel"/>
    <memberTool name="kitsu_publish_capture"/>
  </toolshelf>

  <tool name="kitsu_open_panel" label="Kitsu" icon="kitsu_houdini_plugin">
    <helpText>Open the Kitsu panel</helpText>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[
import hou
import traceback

_INTERFACE_NAME = "kitsu_houdini_plugin"
# Narrow and tall, matching the panel's own stacked-sections content.
# Clamped to the screen in _floating_geometry() below.
_DEFAULT_WIDTH = 460
_DEFAULT_HEIGHT = 640


def _floating_geometry():
    """Return (position, size) for a new floating window on whichever
    screen the cursor is on, or (None, None) to let Houdini place it
    itself. See the top-of-file note on why the default placement is
    worth avoiding where possible."""
    try:
        import uiutils
        from hutil.Qt import QtGui

        mouse_pos = QtGui.QCursor.pos()
        screen_rect = uiutils.getScreenRect(mouse_pos)
        # fitWindowToScreen() only ever MOVES the rect, never shrinks it
        # (its own docstring assumes the size already fits), so clamp first.
        width = min(_DEFAULT_WIDTH, screen_rect.width())
        height = min(_DEFAULT_HEIGHT, screen_rect.height())
        window_rect = uiutils.fitWindowToScreen(mouse_pos, width, height, True)

        # Houdini window positions are bottom-up while Qt's rects are
        # top-down: same flip SideFX's own floating Python Panels apply.
        position = [window_rect.left(), screen_rect.height() - window_rect.bottom()]
        size = [window_rect.width(), window_rect.height()]
        return position, size
    except Exception:
        # Placement is a nicety, not worth failing the whole action over
        # (e.g. QGuiApplication.screenAt() returns None for a cursor that
        # isn't on any screen).
        return None, None


def _reachable_existing_panel(pytype, cur_desktop):
    """Return an already-open Kitsu pane tab the artist can actually SEE,
    or None. A tab that exists but sits on another desktop or in a pane
    collapsed on a split would accept setIsCurrentTab() without error
    while remaining invisible, which is indistinguishable from the shelf
    button doing nothing."""
    for tab in hou.ui.paneTabs():
        if tab.type() != hou.paneTabType.PythonPanel:
            continue
        if tab.activeInterface() != pytype:
            continue
        pane = tab.pane()
        if pane is None:
            # No pane means a stripped-down floating panel, i.e. one left
            # over from the old createFloatingPaneTab() code path. It has
            # no tab strip and can't be docked, but it IS a visible
            # window, so reuse it rather than opening a second one.
            if tab.isFloating():
                return tab
            continue
        if pane.desktop() != cur_desktop:
            continue
        if pane.isSplitMinimized():
            continue
        return tab
    return None


def _raise_window_of(tab):
    """Bring the window containing `tab` to the front. Being the current
    tab isn't enough for a floating window sitting behind Houdini's main
    one."""
    window = None
    floating_panel = tab.floatingPanel()
    if floating_panel is not None:
        window = floating_panel.qtParentWindow()
    if window is None:
        pane = tab.pane()
        if pane is not None:
            window = pane.qtParentWindow()
    if window is not None:
        window.raise_()
        window.activateWindow()


def _open_kitsu_panel():
    # Runs from Houdini's idle callback, NOT inside the shelf click's own
    # call stack (see the executeDeferred() call at the bottom), so it
    # needs its own error reporting: the try/except down there has long
    # since returned by the time this runs. Reported as a dialog because
    # Houdini otherwise surfaces a shelf tool exception only as a small
    # status-bar icon, which looks identical to the button doing nothing.
    try:
        pytype = hou.pypanel.interfaces().get(_INTERFACE_NAME)
        if pytype is None:
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"The '{_INTERFACE_NAME}' Python Panel interface isn't "
                "registered. Check that this project's HOUDINI_PATH "
                "includes its kitsu_houdini_plugin folder."
            )

        cur_desktop = hou.ui.curDesktop()

        existing = _reachable_existing_panel(pytype, cur_desktop)
        if existing is not None:
            existing.setIsCurrentTab()
            _raise_window_of(existing)
            return

        position, size = _floating_geometry()
        geometry = {}
        if position is not None and size is not None:
            geometry["position"] = position
            geometry["size"] = size

        # createFloatingPanel(), not createFloatingPaneTab(): only the
        # former produces a window with a real, draggable tab strip that
        # can be docked into a pane. The interface name is passed here
        # rather than via a separate setActiveInterface() call: one
        # window/GL operation instead of two. See the top-of-file note on
        # both points.
        floating_panel = cur_desktop.createFloatingPanel(
            hou.paneTabType.PythonPanel,
            python_panel_interface=_INTERFACE_NAME,
            **geometry,
        )
        floating_panel.setName("Kitsu")

        # Houdini can hide the tab strip when a pane holds only one tab,
        # which would leave nothing visible to grab to dock this later.
        for pane in floating_panel.panes():
            pane.showPaneTabs(True)
    except Exception as exc:
        hou.ui.displayMessage(
            f"Could not open the Kitsu panel: {exc}",
            severity=hou.severityType.Error,
            title="Kitsu",
            details=traceback.format_exc(),
        )


try:
    import hdefereval
except ImportError:
    # No idle-callback module available (unusual in a GUI session): still
    # better to open the panel synchronously than not at all.
    _open_kitsu_panel()
else:
    hdefereval.executeDeferred(_open_kitsu_panel)
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="kitsu_publish_capture" label="Publish Capture" icon="kitsu_houdini_plugin">
    <helpText>Export a full-scene video capture and publish it to Kitsu as a new revision</helpText>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[
import hou

from kitsu_houdini_plugin import panel, publish_capture

active_panel = panel.get_active_panel()
try:
    publish_capture.ensure_ready(active_panel)
except publish_capture.PublishCaptureError as exc:
    hou.ui.displayMessage(str(exc), title="Kitsu")
else:
    try:
        statuses = active_panel.session.all_task_statuses(active_panel.project)
    except Exception as exc:
        hou.ui.displayMessage(f"Could not load task statuses: {exc}", title="Kitsu")
    else:
        from kitsu_houdini_plugin.capture_dialog import prompt_for_status_and_comment

        prompted = prompt_for_status_and_comment(statuses, parent=hou.qt.mainWindow())
        if prompted is not None:
            task_status, comment = prompted
            try:
                output_path = publish_capture.run(active_panel, task_status, comment)
            except publish_capture.PublishCaptureError as exc:
                hou.ui.displayMessage(str(exc), title="Kitsu")
            except Exception as exc:
                hou.ui.displayMessage(f"Capture export failed: {exc}", title="Kitsu")
            else:
                hou.ui.displayMessage(
                    f"Capture published to Kitsu and saved to:\n{output_path}",
                    title="Kitsu",
                )
]]></script>
  </tool>
</shelfDocument>
